  - id: WizardWrx.HTTP.JSON_Deserialized_Object
    commentId: T:WizardWrx.HTTP.JSON_Deserialized_Object
    language: CSharp
    name:
      CSharp: JSON_Deserialized_Object
      VB: JSON_Deserialized_Object
    nameWithType:
      CSharp: JSON_Deserialized_Object
      VB: JSON_Deserialized_Object
    qualifiedName:
      CSharp: WizardWrx.HTTP.JSON_Deserialized_Object
      VB: WizardWrx.HTTP.JSON_Deserialized_Object
    type: Class
    assemblies:
    - WizardWrx.HTTP
    namespace: WizardWrx.HTTP
    source:
      remote:
        path: HTTP/JSON_Deserialized_Object.cs
        branch: master
        repo: https://github.com/txwizard/WizardWrx_NET_API.git
      id: JSON_Deserialized_Object
      path: ../HTTP/JSON_Deserialized_Object.cs
      startLine: 197
    summary: "\nInstances of this class provide a lightweight, schema-aware façade over\na JSON payload. The class optionally sanitizes the input, validates \nrequired fields, and exposes a minimal set of typed accessors so callers\ncan retrieve field values without depending directly on JSON.NET, working\nwith JToken instances, or needing to expend the effort to parse the JSON\ninto a strongly-typed model when only a few fields are needed.\n"
    remarks: "\n<p>\nThe typed getters intentionally cover only the canonical .NET types\nthat map cleanly and unambiguously to JSON: string, Boolean, 32-bit\ninteger, double-precision floating point, Guid, and DateTime. JSON does\nnot define numeric widths, temporal subtypes, or alternative Boolean\nrepresentations, so additional getters (such as byte, short, long,\nfloat, DateOnly, DateTimeOffset, or truthy/falsy coercions) would not\nprovide meaningful additional capability. Callers who require more\nspecialized interpretations can retrieve the raw string value and apply\ndomain-specific parsing rules.\n</p>\n<p>\nThis design keeps the API surface minimal, deterministic, and\nintention-revealing, while insulating callers from JSON.NET and\npreserving the ability to evolve the internal representation in the\nfuture.\n</p>\n"
    example:
    - "\n<p>\nThe following example shows a typical usage pattern for this class:\n</p>\n\n<pre><code>// Raw JSON received from an external source.\nstring json = @&quot;\n{\n    &quot;id&quot;: &quot;c0a8012e-5f3c-4b8f-9b7a-2f4f0e9c1a12&quot;,\n    &quot;name&quot;: &quot;Alice&quot;,\n    &quot;age&quot;: 42,\n    &quot;active&quot;: true,\n    &quot;created&quot;: &quot;2024-10-12T14:30:00Z&quot;\n}&quot;;\n\n// Define the fields that must be present.\nvar required = new[] { &quot;id&quot;, &quot;name&quot;, &quot;active&quot; };\n\n// Create the wrapper.\nvar wrapper = new JsonFieldAccessor(json, required);\n\n// Validate the payload.\nvar missing = wrapper.Validate(requireNonEmptyValues: true);\nif (missing.Count > 0)\n{\n    Console.WriteLine(&quot;Missing or invalid fields:&quot;);\n    foreach (var field in missing)\n        Console.WriteLine(&quot;  &quot; + field);\n    return;\n}\n\n// Retrieve typed values.\nGuid? id = wrapper.GetFieldValueAsGuid(&quot;id&quot;);\nstring name = wrapper.GetFieldValueAsString(&quot;name&quot;);\nint? age = wrapper.GetFieldValueAsInt(&quot;age&quot;);\nbool? active = wrapper.GetFieldValueAsBoolean(&quot;active&quot;);\nDateTime? created = wrapper.GetFieldValueAsDateTime(&quot;created&quot;);\n\nConsole.WriteLine($&quot;User {name} ({id})&quot;);\nConsole.WriteLine($&quot;Age: {age}&quot;);\nConsole.WriteLine($&quot;Active: {active}&quot;);\nConsole.WriteLine($&quot;Created: {created:u}&quot;);</code></pre>\n\n<p>\nThis example demonstrates validation, typed field retrieval, and the\nabsence of any dependency on JSON.NET in the calling code.\n</p>\n\n<p>\nThe following example demonstrates how to handle optional fields:\n</p>\n\n<pre><code>string json = @&quot;\n{\n    &quot;username&quot;: &quot;bob&quot;,\n    &quot;email&quot;: &quot;bob@example.com&quot;,\n    &quot;age&quot;: &quot;not-a-number&quot;,\n    &quot;lastLogin&quot;: null\n}&quot;;\n\nvar required = new[] { &quot;username&quot; };\nvar wrapper = new JsonFieldAccessor(json, required);\n\n// Validate required fields only.\nvar missing = wrapper.Validate(requireNonEmptyValues: true);\nif (missing.Count > 0)\n    throw new InvalidOperationException(&quot;Required fields missing.&quot;);\n\n// Optional fields: callers decide how to interpret nulls.\nstring email = wrapper.GetFieldValueAsString(&quot;email&quot;);   // &quot;bob@example.com&quot;\nint? age = wrapper.GetFieldValueAsInt(&quot;age&quot;);            // null (invalid integer)\nDateTime? lastLogin = wrapper.GetFieldValueAsDateTime(&quot;lastLogin&quot;); // null\n\nConsole.WriteLine($&quot;User: {email}, Age: {age}, Last Login: {lastLogin}&quot;);</code></pre>\n\n<p>\nThis example shows how optional fields naturally map to nullable return\nvalues, allowing callers to apply their own fallback or defaulting logic.\n</p>\n\n<p>\nThe following example shows how callers can apply domain-specific parsing\nrules when the built-in typed accessors intentionally avoid coercions:\n</p>\n\n<pre><code>string json = @&quot;\n{\n    &quot;enabled&quot;: &quot;yes&quot;,\n    &quot;expires&quot;: &quot;2024-10-12T14:30:00-05:00&quot;,\n    &quot;priority&quot;: &quot;high&quot;\n}&quot;;\n\nvar wrapper = new JsonFieldAccessor(json, Array.Empty&lt;string>());\n\n// Built-in Boolean accessor returns null because &quot;yes&quot; is not a JSON Boolean.\nbool? enabled = wrapper.GetFieldValueAsBoolean(&quot;enabled&quot;);   // null\n\n// Domain-specific truthy/falsy parsing.\nstring rawEnabled = wrapper.GetFieldValueAsString(&quot;enabled&quot;);\nbool enabledFlag = rawEnabled?.Equals(&quot;yes&quot;, StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase) == true;\n\n// Built-in DateTime accessor parses the timestamp but normalizes the offset.\nDateTime? expires = wrapper.GetFieldValueAsDateTime(&quot;expires&quot;);\n\n// Domain-specific offset-preserving parsing.\nstring rawExpires = wrapper.GetFieldValueAsString(&quot;expires&quot;);\nDateTimeOffset dto = DateTimeOffset.Parse(rawExpires);\n\nConsole.WriteLine($&quot;Enabled: {enabledFlag}&quot;);\nConsole.WriteLine($&quot;Expires (DateTime): {expires}&quot;);\nConsole.WriteLine($&quot;Expires (DateTimeOffset): {dto}&quot;);</code></pre>\n\n<p>\nThis example illustrates how callers can retrieve the raw string when they\nneed to apply custom Boolean rules, preserve timezone offsets, or interpret\nvalues according to domain-specific semantics.\n</p>\n"
    syntax:
      content:
        CSharp: public class JSON_Deserialized_Object
        VB: Public Class JSON_Deserialized_Object
